this workout is good, give it to mw as a pdf and add it to my calendarUnderstood. Here is the full "ELITE HOOP CRAFT: IN-SEASON PRECISION & FLOW – 5-Week Basketball Skill Mastery Block" for your review:  PROGRAM HEADER: ELITE HOOP CRAFT: IN-SEASON PRECISION & FLOW – 5-Week Basketball Skill Mastery Block  PROGRAM OVERVIEW: This 5-week in-season skill mastery block is engineered for the elite athlete looking to dominate from every offensive position. The program targets your specific areas for growth: generating separation from a static position, preventing premature ball-killing, enhancing 3-point efficiency, and accelerating your game flow and court mapping. We will systematically build on your midrange shot creation, develop intricate counters, and refine your overall offensive toolkit. Each session is structured to simulate game intensity, integrate advanced footwork (like Jalen Brunson), master change of pace (Shai Gilgeous-Alexander), optimize screen usage (CJ McCollum), expand your versatility (Lauri Markkanen), sharpen your decision-making (LeBron James), and harness your speed with control (Tyrese Maxey). The unique training environment with the tighter hoop will demand unparalleled accuracy and focus, translating directly to elite performance in competition.  WEEKLY STRUCTURE OVERVIEW: This program is designed for 5 training days, allowing for two dedicated rest days (which you've earmarked for film study). Each session will be 90-120 minutes, focused purely on skill development.  Monday: Ball Handling & Static Separation Dominance Tuesday: Mid-Range Mastery & Counter-Attack Wednesday: 3-Point Efficiency & Shot Creation Thursday: Game Flow, Court Mapping & Decision-Making Friday: Post Play, Floaters & Advanced Finishing FILM STUDY GUIDANCE (Your Rest Days): Since you'll be dedicating your rest days to film study, here's a focused approach to maximize your learning from the players you admire, and your own game:  1. General Principles for Watching Elite Players (SGA, Brunson, McCollum, Markkanen, LeBron, Maxey):  Pre-Catch Habits: Watch how they move before they receive the ball. Are they setting screens, cutting, relocating? How do they get open? Patience & Ball Control: Notice how long they hold the ball without forcing action. How do they dribble to maintain advantage, not just to advance the ball? Look for "live dribbles" vs. "dead dribbles." Eyes & Head Swivel (Court Mapping): Trace their eyes. When do they scan the floor? Before receiving the ball, on the catch, during the dribble? How do they anticipate defensive rotations or open teammates? Change of Pace/Direction: Pay close attention to their footwork and dribble rhythm. When do they explode? When do they slow down? How do they use hesitation and re-acceleration? Shot Prep & Footwork: Analyze their footwork into shots – catch-and-shoot, off-the-dribble. How quickly do they get their feet set? How balanced are they? Counters: When a primary move is cut off, what's their immediate counter? What's the counter to the counter? Foul Drawing (SGA): How do they initiate contact? Where do they position their body relative to the defender? How do they use their body for protection and to draw the foul without losing balance or control? Screen Usage (McCollum): How do they set up their defender for the screen? How do they read the screener's defender? Do they go over, under, reject, or use a "split" move? What's their read out of the screen (shot, pass, drive)? Post Footwork (Brunson): Observe his back-to-basket game, how he establishes position, uses drop steps, up-and-unders, and pivots. Note his face-up game and how he creates space. Floater Game (Brunson): Study his approach angles, how he uses his body to protect the ball, the release point, and touch on his floaters. Shot Versatility (Markkanen): How does he get his shot off over different defenders? What footwork does he use for different types of shots (fadeaway, step-back, catch-and-shoot)? Decision Making (LeBron): Pause the film at critical moments. What are all the options? What's the best option? What did LeBron do, and why? Look for his drive-and-kick, post-up reads, and transition vision. Speed Control (Maxey): How does he use his speed to gain an advantage without rushing or losing control? When does he accelerate into space, and when does he decelerate to maintain possession or set up a move? 2. Analyzing Your Own Game Film:  Static Position Struggles: Record yourself moving from a static position. Is your first step explosive? Are you telegraphing your moves? Are you using proper jab series and fakes? Killing the Ball: Watch your possessions. How often do you pick up your dribble without a clear advantage or next move? Are you scanning the court before dribbling into trouble? 3-Point Attempts: Analyze your form. Are you balanced? Consistent release point? Are you rushing your shot? How does defensive pressure affect your form? Game Flow/Court Mapping: Track your eye movement. Are you seeing the whole floor? Are you missing open teammates or rotations? Are you too focused on the ball? Decision-Making: After a play, pause and ask: What was the primary read? What were the secondary reads? Did I make the optimal decision given the defense? Energy & Body Language: How do you look when things aren't going your way? How do you react to mistakes? Elite players maintain composure and focus. SESSION-BY-SESSION BREAKDOWN DAY 1: Ball Handling & Static Separation Dominance Objective: Develop explosive first-step quickness, master multiple moves from a static position, and cultivate a tight, game-ready handle for creating offensive advantage.  Warm-Up (15 minutes):  Dynamic Stretching (5 min): Arm circles (forward/backward), leg swings (front/side), torso twists, walking knee hugs, walking quad stretch. Ball Familiarity & Stationary Dribbles (5 min): Finger-tip taps, figure-8 around legs, pound dribbles (high/low), crossover, between-legs, behind-back. Focus on keeping eyes up. Footwork Prep (5 min): Quick Feet Drills: Line hops (forward/backward, lateral), "Ickey Shuffle" through imaginary cones. (30 sec each, 2 rounds) Explosive Step Prep: 3-step approach to a jump (no ball), focus on quick ground contact and balance. (5 reps each side) Main Block (75-90 minutes):  Explosive First Step & Static Separation Series (30 minutes):  Jab Series to Drive (3x8-10 reps each side): Start static, triple threat. Jab-and-Go: Hard jab, explode past imaginary defender. Jab-Cross-Go: Hard jab, bring ball back, crossover, explode. Jab-Hesitation-Go: Hard jab, fake hesitation, explode. Progression: Introduce a cone as defender, dribble around it. Coaching Cues: "Sell the jab with your eyes and shoulders." "Low man wins – explode forward, not up." "First step eats space." Crossover & Through the Legs from Static (3x8-10 reps each side): Start static, then execute a hard crossover or through-the-legs move, exploding into 1-2 dribbles. Progression: Combine with a retreat dribble and re-attack. Coaching Cues: "Attack the defender's lead foot." "Stay low, protect the ball." "The change of direction is sudden and violent." "SGA" Change of Pace Dribble Attack (3x5 reps each side): Start static with a dribble. Slow, almost walking pace, then explode into a drive with 1-2 hard dribbles, simulating drawing contact. Progression: Add a shoulder bump at the end of the drive. Coaching Cues: "Control the tempo. Lull the defender, then burst." "Lower your shoulder into the imaginary contact." "Finish strong, anticipate the foul." Advanced Ball Handling for Flow & Protection (30 minutes):  Pound Dribbles with Change of Direction (33x1 min each): High Pounds (waist high), Crossover, Behind-Back, Between-Legs. Low Pounds (ankle high), Crossover, Behind-Back, Between-Legs. Progression: Perform while moving laterally within your 12-18ft space. Coaching Cues: "Dribble through the floor, not on top of it." "Protect the ball with your body." "Keep your eyes up, scan the 'court'." Combo Moves Series (3x1 min each): Crossover-Behind-Back Between-Legs-Crossover Double Crossover Progression: Transition these moves into an explosive 1-2 dribble attack towards the hoop, aiming for the precise 30cm rim. Coaching Cues: "Fluidity and quickness between moves." "Don't pause. Link the dribbles." "Finish at the rim with touch, adapting to the tight target." "Maxey" Speed Dribbles with Control (3x30 sec each): Sprint dribble across your space, then sudden stop and controlled pivot or crossover. Progression: Add a quick pass motion (fake pass) out of the stop. Coaching Cues: "Max speed, but with maximum control. Don't let the ball get ahead of you." "Sudden stop, instant balance." "Scan the 'floor' during your speed dribble." Finishers: Tight Space Layups & Touch Shots (15-30 minutes):  Controlled Power Layups (3x10 each side): Dribble from a static position, 1-2 dribbles, controlled layup off the glass/straight in. Focus on using minimal space. Progression: Add a small contact bump into the finish. Coaching Cues: "High release point." "Soft touch on the narrow rim." "Use the backboard angle, even with the tight target." "Brunson" Floaters (3x10 each side): 1-2 dribbles into a floater from various angles within 5-8 feet. Focus on arc and soft touch. Progression: Incorporate a slight hesitation dribble before the floater. Coaching Cues: "High arc is your friend." "Soft hands, kiss it off the fingertips." "Protect the ball from imaginary shot blockers." Cool-Down (10 minutes):  Static Stretches: Hamstring stretch, quad stretch, calf stretch, groin stretch, triceps stretch. Hold each for 20-30 seconds. Deep Breathing: 2 minutes of controlled diaphragmatic breathing. DAY 2: Mid-Range Mastery & Counter-Attack Objective: Develop a versatile midrange game with multiple scoring options, including pull-ups, step-backs, and fadeaways, and integrate effective counter moves.  Warm-Up (15 minutes):  Dynamic Stretching (5 min): Same as Day 1. Form Shooting (5 min): 10